WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.433 --> 00:00:02.435
-You know
our first guest tonight

00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:03.636
from HBO's hit series "Girls"

00:00:03.636 --> 00:00:04.804
and the critically acclaimed
film "Get Out."

00:00:04.804 --> 00:00:07.206
She stars in and executive
produced "M3GAN,"

00:00:07.206 --> 00:00:08.341
which is in theaters now.

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:09.542
Let's take a look.

00:00:09.542 --> 00:00:12.345
-Being a parent was never
in the cards for you.

00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:17.917
You're a beautiful, creative,
strong, ambitious young woman.

00:00:17.917 --> 00:00:20.452
Your first love is always
gonna be your career,

00:00:20.452 --> 00:00:22.955
and you shouldn't have to
feel guilty about that.

00:00:22.955 --> 00:00:25.959
Let me focus on Cady
so that you can focus on

00:00:25.959 --> 00:00:29.763
the things that matter most
to you.

00:00:29.763 --> 00:00:31.864
-M3GAN, do you see this pen?

00:00:31.864 --> 00:00:35.001
-You know something, Gemma?
You're exhausting.

00:00:35.001 --> 00:00:37.470
Now, I can either do this
with you or without you,

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:39.605
but I'm not gonna waste any more
time discussing it.

00:00:39.605 --> 00:00:41.174
-Gemma?

00:00:42.742 --> 00:00:45.577
-[ In Gemma's voice ]
Katie, don't come in here.

00:00:45.577 --> 00:00:47.246
-Please welcome back to
the show,

00:00:47.246 --> 00:00:49.649
our friend,
Allison Williams, everybody!

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:52.518
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:52.518 --> 00:00:57.890 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:03.296 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:03.296 --> 00:01:06.498
-Allison,
I'm so happy to see you!

00:01:06.498 --> 00:01:07.900
-I'm so happy to see you!

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:10.236
-It's been -- obviously, a lot
has happened in the world,

00:01:10.236 --> 00:01:12.104
but it's been a long time
since you've been here,

00:01:12.104 --> 00:01:13.472
and a lot has happened
for you as well.

00:01:13.472 --> 00:01:15.975
I want to talk about the movie,
but you had a baby.

00:01:15.975 --> 00:01:18.711
-What?
-Yes, you did!

00:01:18.711 --> 00:01:20.046
-I did.
-A real one,

00:01:20.046 --> 00:01:22.949
not some creepy M3GAN,
a real, actual human baby.

00:01:22.949 --> 00:01:25.184
-I had a real, human baby, yeah.

00:01:25.184 --> 00:01:26.752
-And engaged as well.
Congratulations on everything.

00:01:26.752 --> 00:01:28.587
-Thank you so much. Thank you.

00:01:28.587 --> 00:01:30.489
-So I've been lucky
over the years

00:01:30.489 --> 00:01:32.825
to get to know your parents
pretty well,

00:01:32.825 --> 00:01:35.094
and I know they took
being a parent very seriously.

00:01:35.094 --> 00:01:36.963
They were incredibly loving
and supportive.

00:01:36.963 --> 00:01:39.532
-When he says that, by the way,
what he means is that

00:01:39.532 --> 00:01:41.901
every time he saw my dad
in the buildings --

00:01:41.901 --> 00:01:44.037
my dad used to work for NBC --
my dad would say,

00:01:44.037 --> 00:01:45.438
"You know my daughter
wants to be an actress."

00:01:45.438 --> 00:01:48.073
And you were like,
"It's not up to me."

00:01:48.073 --> 00:01:49.275
-Yeah, there's only --

00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:51.110
And in fact,
I'm gonna say this now,

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:52.578
because it makes what
you've accomplished

00:01:52.578 --> 00:01:54.947
even more impressive --
I did everything to stop it.

00:01:54.947 --> 00:01:56.282
[ Laughter ]
-You did?

00:01:56.282 --> 00:01:58.685
-Thwarted. Thwarted again!

00:01:58.685 --> 00:02:00.253
-And I couldn't manage it.
-You couldn't stop it.

00:02:00.253 --> 00:02:01.454
It was inevitable.

00:02:01.454 --> 00:02:03.890
-My question was gonna be,
how are they doing

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:04.924
as grandparents?

00:02:04.924 --> 00:02:06.058
-Oh, they're obsessed with it.

00:02:06.058 --> 00:02:09.729
I guess it's like a drug,
grandparent-ism?

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:11.063
Grandparent-hood?
-Yeah.

00:02:11.063 --> 00:02:13.166
-It's like,
intoxicating in this way,

00:02:13.166 --> 00:02:16.135
because it's all the love
and none of the responsibility.

00:02:16.135 --> 00:02:19.072
And so they just --
they're so into it.

00:02:19.072 --> 00:02:21.841
They're so engaged. They're
super helpful. It's the best.

00:02:21.841 --> 00:02:24.710
They're funny.
They know all the songs still.

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:27.279
They know "Head, Shoulders,
Knees and Toes" like it was
yesterday.

00:02:27.279 --> 00:02:29.681
-Well, there's a lot of clues
within the song

00:02:29.681 --> 00:02:31.216
as to where ---
-That's true.

00:02:31.216 --> 00:02:33.786
-It's one of the easier
choreographies to remember.

00:02:33.786 --> 00:02:36.523
-But the bridge has, you know,
eyes and ears and nose.

00:02:36.523 --> 00:02:37.824
-Oh, yeah.
I never get to the bridge.

00:02:37.824 --> 00:02:39.659
-Oh, you don't? You skip.
You're just a verse guy.

00:02:39.659 --> 00:02:42.028
-I'm just like, "We're just
gonna do the main parts.

00:02:42.028 --> 00:02:43.362
Chorus is for high school."

00:02:43.362 --> 00:02:46.632
-You're probably like a
one-animal "Old MacDonald."

00:02:46.632 --> 00:02:48.334
-Oh, absolutely.
-Oh, gosh.

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:49.435
We do the whole -- anyway.

00:02:49.435 --> 00:02:52.104
They're very, very engaged
and amazing.

00:02:52.104 --> 00:02:53.239
I'm really lucky.

00:02:53.239 --> 00:02:56.208
-You had,
your family came to you,

00:02:56.208 --> 00:02:58.644
to the premiere of "M3GAN."
-Yes, that's true.

00:02:58.644 --> 00:03:00.179
-This is a lovely photo
of everybody.

00:03:00.179 --> 00:03:01.380
-Yes.
-And I want to show

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:03.616
a second photo as well,
'cause here you are.

00:03:03.616 --> 00:03:05.284
This is a great
red carpet photo

00:03:05.284 --> 00:03:08.054
with a bunch of M3GANs.

00:03:08.054 --> 00:03:10.056
Also your dad,
and I should note,

00:03:10.056 --> 00:03:11.924
this is the producer of the
film,
Jason Blum.

00:03:11.924 --> 00:03:14.060
-That's my boss.
-Yeah.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:16.595
So you have sort of four
M3GAN look-a-likes

00:03:16.595 --> 00:03:18.031
and then a look-not-alike.

00:03:18.031 --> 00:03:19.565
[ Laughing ]

00:03:19.565 --> 00:03:22.167
But the part I just noticed is
that here's --

00:03:22.167 --> 00:03:24.303
look how happy your dad is,
here he is,

00:03:24.303 --> 00:03:26.138
to be with his family.
-Yeah.

00:03:26.138 --> 00:03:28.474
-And then here's how happy he is
to be with the M3GANs.

00:03:28.474 --> 00:03:31.710
[ Laughter ]

00:03:32.578 --> 00:03:36.983
So this movie had
a fantastic first weekend,

00:03:36.983 --> 00:03:39.419
which is so exciting,
because I think, you know,

00:03:39.419 --> 00:03:41.153
anybody who loves movie,
it's really exciting when

00:03:41.153 --> 00:03:43.722
people want to go back out to
the theater to see them,

00:03:43.722 --> 00:03:46.058
and obviously, you have
the Avatars of the world,

00:03:46.058 --> 00:03:47.793
which are sort of
a different thing,

00:03:47.793 --> 00:03:50.463
but this is a movie people were
so excited to go to.

00:03:50.463 --> 00:03:53.132
It was PG-13, so like,
just people --

00:03:53.132 --> 00:03:54.633
I think this was a movie that

00:03:54.633 --> 00:03:56.769
people loved the idea of
seeing it in a theater.

00:03:56.769 --> 00:03:58.471
What was it like being
at the premiere with

00:03:58.471 --> 00:03:59.906
a packed house?
-So fun.

00:03:59.906 --> 00:04:02.941
Well, of course I've spent
months watching cuts of it,

00:04:02.941 --> 00:04:04.843
just like, on an iPad,
which is, it was great.

00:04:04.843 --> 00:04:07.847
I was excited about it,
but seeing it in a full theater

00:04:07.847 --> 00:04:11.050
tells you a lot about
humans and human nature,

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:12.384
because about halfway through
our movie,

00:04:12.384 --> 00:04:14.586
something truly
messed up happens,

00:04:14.586 --> 00:04:16.823
and people were cheering.

00:04:16.823 --> 00:04:18.090
And it wasn't
an isolated incident.

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:19.224
It happened again.

00:04:19.224 --> 00:04:20.626
I went to another screening
in New York,

00:04:20.626 --> 00:04:22.528
full audience, people cheer,

00:04:22.528 --> 00:04:24.931
and then there's, like,
a self-loathing laughter

00:04:24.931 --> 00:04:26.332
that happens afterwards
where you're like,

00:04:26.332 --> 00:04:29.101
"I can't believe
every part of my being,

00:04:29.101 --> 00:04:30.669
all cells of my organism

00:04:30.669 --> 00:04:32.138
were rooting for
this thing to happen."

00:04:32.138 --> 00:04:34.673
And people are like, ugh. Ew!

00:04:34.673 --> 00:04:36.342
They, like,
want to shower afterward.

00:04:36.342 --> 00:04:37.777
But that's what's so fun
about this movie.

00:04:37.777 --> 00:04:39.145
It's fun.

00:04:39.145 --> 00:04:40.412
People come out of it and say,
"I had a blast,"

00:04:40.412 --> 00:04:41.547
which sounds weird,

00:04:41.547 --> 00:04:43.148
'cause you're also
scared the whole time.

00:04:43.148 --> 00:04:45.884
But it's funny and it's like --

00:04:45.884 --> 00:04:48.387
it's self-aware in a weird way.

00:04:48.387 --> 00:04:50.322
But yeah, of course,
we were thrilled

00:04:50.322 --> 00:04:51.758
that audiences were having
so much fun with it.

00:04:51.758 --> 00:04:53.659
-I think that at this point,

00:04:53.659 --> 00:04:54.893
if people are gonna
make that leap

00:04:54.893 --> 00:04:57.062
to actually go out of the house
and to see it.

00:04:57.062 --> 00:04:58.964
-If you're gonna leave
and pay the $1,000 per ticket...

00:04:58.964 --> 00:05:03.536
and do the whole thing,
masks or no masks

00:05:03.536 --> 00:05:05.638
or whatever you want to do,
it's a big ask.

00:05:05.638 --> 00:05:07.440
-Yeah.
-So I better mean it

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:09.208
when I say you're gonna
have fun, and I do.

00:05:09.208 --> 00:05:10.977
I mean it.
You're gonna enjoy it.

00:05:10.977 --> 00:05:13.012
-The film was made by people
who understand

00:05:13.012 --> 00:05:14.814
that people are gonna
see it together.

00:05:14.814 --> 00:05:18.117
I think that self-awareness
means that, like,

00:05:18.117 --> 00:05:20.286
look, we know exactly
who this is for,

00:05:20.286 --> 00:05:22.254
and that's why I think when
people laugh and people scream

00:05:22.254 --> 00:05:24.457
at the same time, part of it
is in appreciation of,

00:05:24.457 --> 00:05:26.025
"Oh, the people who made this
movie took care of us."

00:05:26.025 --> 00:05:27.393
-Yeah, we made it for you.
-Yeah.

00:05:27.393 --> 00:05:29.428
-Like, we built in time for you
to have the responses

00:05:29.428 --> 00:05:31.163
that we hoped you
were gonna have.

00:05:31.163 --> 00:05:32.498
We had to add time.

00:05:32.498 --> 00:05:34.566
At one point --
M3GAN likes to sing and dance

00:05:34.566 --> 00:05:35.901
sort of spontaneously,

00:05:35.901 --> 00:05:38.137
and one of the moments where
she breaks into song,

00:05:38.137 --> 00:05:41.040
we had to add time, because
the laughter in test screenings

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:43.008
took too long,
so that's the kind of thing.

00:05:43.008 --> 00:05:44.810
We're making it for you guys.

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:46.145
We're not just making it
for ourselves.

00:05:46.145 --> 00:05:48.414
I mean, we're all vain,
obviously.

00:05:48.414 --> 00:05:50.850
But it's not that vain.
-It helps a great deal.

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:52.351
-Exactly, exactly.
-We make it for ourselves,

00:05:52.351 --> 00:05:53.986
but then we realized --
-Other people are gonna see it.

00:05:53.986 --> 00:05:55.588
-Yeah, because we like to
make it for ourselves,

00:05:55.588 --> 00:05:57.056
but then we also like to
make money.

00:05:57.056 --> 00:06:00.192
-Exactly, and there's
our whole industry.

00:06:00.192 --> 00:06:02.061
-We just described showbiz.
-That's showbiz.

00:06:02.061 --> 00:06:05.097
-I have so many more questions
for Allison.

00:06:05.097 --> 00:06:06.699
We'll be right back with more
after this.

